--TEST--
`Class::method(...)` and `$obj->method(...)` build an ordinary `callable`, over a static target, an instance one, an overridden one and a `Core` member alike — `rule:types/callable-values`, `rule:types/anonymous-function` and `rule:types/implicit-capture`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/callable-values`: the `(...)` names the member rather than calling it, and its
// value is a callable bound to it. `rule:types/anonymous-function` makes `callable` the only
// function type, so what these produce is the same object an anonymous function produces.
interface Shape {
    public function area(): int;
}

class Box implements Shape {
    public int $side;

    public function constructor(int $side) {
        $this->side = $side;
    }

    public function area(): int {
        return $this->side * $this->side;
    }

    // `rule:types/implicit-capture`: the receiver is captured by value at the point the
    // `(...)` is evaluated, exactly as an anonymous function captures a binding.
    public function twice(): int {
        callable $own = $this->area(...);
        return ($own() as int) * 2;
    }

    public static function label(): string {
        return "box";
    }

    // `self::` over a static member binds the declaring class, and needs no
    // receiver to remember.
    public static function labelled(): tainted string {
        callable $named = self::label(...);
        return $named() as string;
    }
}

class Slab extends Box {
    public function area(): int {
        return $this->side * 2;
    }
}

// A static target: no receiver, the called class baked in.
callable $shout = Box::label(...);
echo $shout() as string, "\n";

// An instance target, reached through the declaring class.
Box $b = new Box(3);
callable $area = $b->area(...);
echo $area() as int, "\n";
echo $b->twice(), "\n";
echo Box::labelled(), "\n";

// The receiver's own class decides which body runs, as it does at a call:
// `$s` is declared `Shape` and is really a `Slab`.
Shape $s = new Slab(5);
callable $virtual = $s->area(...);
echo $virtual() as int, "\n";

// A Tier 0 `Core` member is native Rust behind a helper symbol, and takes
// the same shape.
callable $upper = Core\Str::upper(...);
echo $upper("shout") as string, "\n";

// The whole point of the syntax: a `Core` member that takes a callback
// cannot tell one of these from a written anonymous function. The reference carries
// `abs`'s own signature (`rule:types/callable-signature`), so `map`'s
// element type is what `abs` answers and the subject must be something
// `abs` accepts.
array<int> $sides = [1, 2, 3];
array<int|float|decimal> $doubled = Core\Arr::map($sides, Core\Math::abs(...));
foreach ($doubled as mixed $one) {
    echo $one, " ";
}
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
box
9
18
box
10
SHOUT
1 2 3
